// D1 round 7 / R7a RULE 1, THE FINDING VERBATIM — the loan must survive the
// HOP through a named reborrow.
//
// The rule (`collect_result_roots` in src/compiler/borrow_check.cpp) adds the
// REFERENT of a named reborrow to the roots a call result inherits from. Its
// defect was measured on exactly this program: the reborrow `r -> s` IS
// recorded and DOES extend s's NLL live range, so the loan is not lost for
// lack of a live range — what never happened is INHERITANCE across the hop.
// The walk handed back the terminal name `r`, `b` co-held only the loan OF
// `s`, and the record `target=c holder=s co=[]` was RELEASED at the `pull()`,
// one line before `c.bump()`:
//
//   line=20 target=c holder=s co=[]  lu=20 RELEASE   (the leak)
//   line=19 target=c holder=s co=[b] lu=21 keep      (the alias-free twin)
//
// so `c.bump()` was ADMITTED and `*b.p` read through a pointer into a `c` that
// had been mutated under it. THE TWO TWINS THAT MAKE THIS A DEFECT AND NOT A
// DESIGN: the same program with the reborrow spelled away — `s.pull()`
// directly, or `(&mut s).pull()` inline — REFUSED. Only the NAMED spelling
// laundered the loan, so the verdict depended on what the program SPELLS.
//
// THE ADMIT HALVES, which is what separates this from an over-refusing
// checker printing the same words: pass/bc_d1r7_a1_no_loan_admit (a reborrow
// of a binding that holds no loan of `c` inherits nothing) and
// pass/bc_d1r7_a1_scope_release_admit (the loan still dies at scope exit).
// The generated-type spelling — the ADR 0025 customer — is
// fail/bc_d1r7_a1_family_next_batch_reborrow.
package bc_d1r7_a1_pull_through_reborrow;

#[borrow_carrying]
struct B { p: &i64 }
#[borrow_carrying]
struct S { p: &i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }
impl C {
    fn mk_s(&self) -> S { return S { p: &self.v }; }
    fn bump(&mut self) { self.v = self.v + 1i64; }
}
impl S {
    fn pull(&mut self) -> B { return B { p: self.p }; }
    fn peek(&self) -> B { return B { p: self.p }; }
}
fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let mut s: S = c.mk_s();         // `s` holds c's loan
    let mut r: &mut S = &mut s;      // the HOP: `r` IS `s`
    let b: B = r.pull();             // …so `b` must inherit c's loan THROUGH it
    c.bump();                        // must refuse: `b` is read below
    return *b.p;
}
